<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/wolfram2002-ch12-51--gpt> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/LLMClaimAssessment>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "GPT-5.6 Sol on wolfram2002-ch12-51: not-empirically-resolvable, resolvability 0.30";
    dc:description "Physical and computational data could support selected operation-count estimates, but the stated comparison has no invariant measurement. The conclusion about evidence of intelligence is epistemic and cannot be decided solely by observing sophisticated computation.";
    dc:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0>;
    dc:type "resolution-route/none";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Proposed test with existing data: One could define a physical computation metric and estimate it for stellar turbulence, human computing, and prime-generating processes, but no standard metric makes these unlike systems commensurable or turns computational quantity into evidence about intelligence.";
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-gpt-5.6-sol-medium>;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/assessmentType> "dataset-resolvability/0.2.0";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/evidenceAgainst> "The human-history comparison changes radically with the cost model.", "There is no agreed measure of computation performed by a physical process.", "Whether an observation counts as evidence of intelligence depends on priors and context.";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasConfidence> "0.84"^^xsd:double;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasScore> "0.298"^^xsd:double;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasVerdict> "not-empirically-resolvable".
